# Linux Fundamentals 2

## Lab Overview
This lab will explore basic linux commands, usage and security relevance

## Objectives
- Understand the commands relevance to IT systems and their security
- Learn how to use these commands in combination with other functions
- Identify the syntax and purposes of the covered commands

## Key Concepts
| Ubuntu | Linux distro. OS built around Linux. Used for desktops, laptops, servers, cloud systems. | 
| shell | Command interpreter. Program that takes text-based commands then tells OS to run them. Handles variables. Supports Scripting. Allows for use redirections(>) and pipes (|)|
| echo | Display text or values in a terminal environment. Show the value of a variable. Write text into a file. |
| string | Sequence of characters(letters, numbers, spaces, symbols) treated as a singular value. Used for names, messages, file paths, variables, command output. |
| ls | Lists the contents of a directory. Used with flags such as: -l, -a, -la. Can show file details, hidden files. |
| cat | Displays the contents of a file inside the terminal. Allows viewing of multiple files, creating a quick file and combining files. Allows quick inspection of files, logs, notes, configurations.
| pwd | Print working directory. Shows full path of the directory a user is currently in. |
| find | locate files/directories based on name, type, size, permissions, owner, modification time. |



## Lab Walkthrough

### Syntax: echo

tryhackme@linux1:~$ echo Hello
Hello

- tryhackme@linux1:~$(shell prompt)
- tryhackme(represents the current user in the shell prompt)
- "~"(means the curent directory is the user's home directory)
- "$"(the current user is a regular user, has no admin privilege)
- "echo Hello"(commanded the shell to print the word "hello")
- "Hello" (the output of the command line "echo Hello")

### Syntax: echo "Hello Friend!"

tryhackme@linux1:~$ echo "Hello Friend!"
Hello Friend!

- echo printed the text inside the quotes
- terminal showed Hello Friend!
- quotes keep the text together as one string
- quotes may be useful when strings include special characters or spaces 

### Syntax: whoami

tryhackme@linux1:~$ whoami
tryhackme

- command asked the system "which user am I currently logged in as?"
- output details current logged-in username, which account commands are running under and current security based on the permissions of the user.
- access to files and commands depend on the user privileges

### Syntax: ls

tryhackme@linux1:~$ ls
'Important Files' 'My Documents' Notes Pictures

- listed the items in the current directory "~"(Important Files, My documents, Notes, Pictures)

### Syntax: ls -l

tryhackme@linux1:~$ ls -l
total 80
-rw-rw-r-- 1 tryhackme tryhackme 65522 May 10  2021 access.log
drwxr-xr-x 2 tryhackme tryhackme  4096 May 10  2021 folder1
drwxr-xr-x 2 tryhackme tryhackme  4096 May 10  2021 folder2
drwxr-xr-x 2 tryhackme tryhackme  4096 May 10  2021 folder3
drwxr-xr-x 2 tryhackme tryhackme  4096 May 10  2021 folder4

- -rw-rw-r--(file type and permissions)
  - "-"(regular file)
  - "rw-"(owner can read/write)
  - "rw-"(group can read/write)
  - "r--"(others can only read)
- (1) hard link count
- tryhackme(owner)
- tryhackme(group)
- 65522(size of the file in bytes)
- May 10 2021(last time modified)
- access.log(file name)
- drwxr-xr-x(file and permissions)
  - "d"(directory)
  - "rwx"(owner can read/write/enter)
  - "r-x"(group can read/enter)
  - "r-x"(others can read/enter)
- total 80(total disk space used by the listed entries in that directory listing
- this output shows who owns the file, who can modify it
- may indicate files or directories that are too broadly accessible

### Syntax: ls -a

tryhackme@linux1:~$ ls -a
.   .Xauthority    .bash_logout  .cache    access.log  folder2  folder4
..  .bash_history  .bashrc       .profile  folder1     folder3

- "."(current directory)
- ".."(parent directory)
- .bashrc, .profile, .bash_history(hiddenfiles)
  - .bash_history(commands previously typed)
  - .bashrc(shell configuration file)
  - .profile(user environment/profile settings)
  - .bash_logout(commands/actions when logging out)
  - .Xauthority(related to graphical session authentication)
  - .cache(cached data)
- .cache(hidden directory)
- access.log, folder1, folder2....(normal visible entries)
- files beginning with "." are hidden(not secret) by default, in normal ls output

