### Syntax: wc -1 access.log

tryhackme@linux1:~$ wc -l access.log
244 access.log

- counted the number of lines in access.log
- file has (244) lines
- "wc"(word count command)
- "-l"(count lines
- useful for quickly seeing how many entries are in a text file or log

### Syntax: grep "81.143.211.90" access.log

tryhackme@linux1:~$ grep "81.143.211.90" access.log
81.143.211.90 - - [25/Mar/2021:11:17 + 0000] "GET / HTTP/1.1" 200 417 "-" "Mozilla/5.0 (Linux; Android 7.0; Moto G(4))"

- grep command searched access.log, for the text "81.143.211.90"
- found (1) maching line
- [25/Mar/2021:11:17 +0000](date and time of the request)
- GET / HTTP/1.1(client requested website root page "/"
- 200(server responded successfully)
- 417(size of the response in bytes)
- "-"(no referrer code)
- Mozilla/5.0...(user-agent string, describing client software/device)

### Syntax: grep -R "PRETTY_NAME" /etc/

grep -R "PRETTY_NAME" /etc/
grep: /etc/sudoers: Permission denied
/etc/os-release:PRETTY_NAME="Ubuntu"

- grep searched through /etc/(recursively using the -R option), for the text "PRETTY_NAME"
- found the character string in /etc/os-release
- grep tried to read /etc/sudoers
- current user does not have permission to read
- skipped file
- /etc(stores configuration files, OS info, services, users, networking, security settings)

## Security Relevance
- These commands can be used for navigation and investigation
- When looking through large amounts of information it could take a lot of time to search each line in a log.
- It is best to isolate and investigate exactly what I am looking for. Commands such as grep  will allow me to look for specific informaiton
